00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.150
So you'll have to apologize,
but this is, in fact,

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:04.710
our real Google Cloud bill.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.440
This is our top level
bill, daily cost

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.460
starting in January 1, 2017
through until February of 2019.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.730
This is as much data as
the built in Google Cloud

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.287
billing console would give us.

00:03:16.287 --> 00:03:18.370
Now because of some of
these spikes in variations,

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.150
it may not be extremely
obvious, but this is actually

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.280
a 3x increase over two years.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.557
So this is 75% annual growth.

00:03:25.557 --> 00:03:27.390
Now for me, this is a
great problem to have.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.340
As an engineer, I
love this because this

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.673
means we've got to go now and
figure out how to optimize

00:03:31.673 --> 00:03:32.520
this and solve this.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.312
It also means that our
business is growing.

00:03:34.312 --> 00:03:36.970
So this is actually a
really good problem to have.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.637
But we do want to make
sure that our growth is

00:03:39.637 --> 00:03:41.220
in line with our
business, or ideally,

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.770
actually grows slower than our
actual growth in our business,

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:44.270
right?

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:47.020
Which would improve the
profitability of the business.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.608
So because this
has happened, we've

00:03:48.608 --> 00:03:51.150
needed to start looking into
all of these questions, like OK,

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:52.460
so why is our bill so high?

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.770
Where is that money going?

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.740
The top level cost like
this doesn't actually

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:57.940
show you a whole
lot of information,

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:00.315
besides hey, you're spending
more money than you used to.

00:04:00.315 --> 00:04:01.648
But I do think it's interesting.

00:04:01.648 --> 00:04:03.850
There are a few interesting
trends you can see here.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:07.270
So at this far left mark here
at around November or December

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:09.910
of 2017 is Black Friday.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.530
We have a very
seasonal business.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:14.200
People do a lot of shopping
starting between Thanksgiving

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:15.680
and the end of the year.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.700
And so that spike there is
their increase for 2017.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.470
We have an extremely
large peak there,

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:21.640
which is in about October.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.140
That was when we went and did
load testing for Black Friday.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:25.682
We want to make sure
that every year,

00:04:25.682 --> 00:04:27.670
we can handle the load
that we're going to see.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:30.035
We go and try to
break our own systems.

00:04:30.035 --> 00:04:31.910
It turns out that cost
a little bit of money.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.070
So that's what that
particular spike

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:34.600
is for on that particular day.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.430
And then you can see
Black Friday 2018.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:37.450
So you can see
here for Bluecore,

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.875
we tend to have this thing
where our costs kind of go up,

00:04:39.875 --> 00:04:42.250
and then they go up a heck of
a lot starting in November.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:43.570
And then they kind of
stay at that level.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:45.153
And then they go up
the next November,

00:04:45.153 --> 00:04:48.250
and then they kind of
stay at that level.

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:48.890
OK.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:49.390
Great.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:52.150
So when we had these questions
about cost when we were smaller

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:53.890
with about 10
software engineers,

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:56.420
we would just go to the all
hands meeting and be like, hey,

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.330
where did our cost
increase come from?

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.390
Last Thursday, something
must have happened.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.590
Our data store cost is up.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.780
Our app engine cost
is up, whatever.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:05.980
Did somebody do something?

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.105
And usually, somebody there
would be like, oh, yep.

00:05:08.105 --> 00:05:10.092
I think we turned on
this new data feature.

00:05:10.092 --> 00:05:11.800
Let me go looking to
see if that's what's

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:13.150
causing the cost increase.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.728
We could go find it
and solve the problem.

00:05:15.728 --> 00:05:18.270
Now that we're bigger, we have
about 40 or so engineers, that

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:19.778
doesn't actually work anymore.

00:05:19.778 --> 00:05:21.820
We have too many changes
and too much complexity.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:23.987
So when we go and ask that
question at the all hands

00:05:23.987 --> 00:05:25.920
meeting, people are
just like, I've no idea.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.630
We changed all sorts of things.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.100
Maybe one of our
customers started

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.100
using a feature in a
new way that changed

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.812
how our application runs.

00:05:34.812 --> 00:05:36.270
So we needed to go
and do something

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.690
to answer these questions.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:42.637
So cloud providers
like Google provide you

00:05:42.637 --> 00:05:43.470
this nice advantage.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.087
There's really,
really clear pricing.

00:05:45.087 --> 00:05:47.670
Go look at the price sheet to
go see what something costs you.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:51.630
And they provide you this nice
detailed bill every single day

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:54.930
to tell you what you actually
use and what things cost.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.630
Before I started at Bluecore,
I came from an organization

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:58.977
that owned its own servers.

00:05:58.977 --> 00:06:00.810
And so I kind of assumed
that this would not

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:02.460
be a problem in
the cloud universe

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.560
because you have this
nice detailed bill.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:06.143
You want to know
what something costs?

00:06:06.143 --> 00:06:07.800
Just look at your bill.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.190
It turns out that that is true
for things that are separate.

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:14.253
So for example, if you have
fairly separable applications,

00:06:14.253 --> 00:06:16.170
and you can have, say,
a separate Google Cloud

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:19.335
project for your team
or for your feature,

00:06:19.335 --> 00:06:21.210
Google's built-in bill
probably will give you

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:22.620
the level of detail you need.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.030
It will tell you, hey,
this team cost this much.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.330
Here's all the line items
that this team actually used.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:30.930
And that may actually
be sufficient.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:32.957
So if that works for
you, that's great.

00:06:32.957 --> 00:06:34.790
But there are things
that have to be shared.

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:36.690
In our case, Bluecore
is a software

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:37.980
as a service application.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.800
So at least it's some sort
of high level logical level.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.000
We have one application that is
used by all of our customers.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.750
And so we end up with
some fairly big line

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.190
items because of that.

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:48.690
There's other good
examples of this.

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:50.910
One that I really like is
like your data warehouse

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:52.180
for your organization.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:53.220
The whole point of
your data warehouse

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.620
is to put all the data from
your various different pieces

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:59.700
into one database that then
everyone in your organization

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.120
can access to be able to learn
the insight that they need to.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.700
By some definition, that has
to be like a shared resource

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.642
for your whole organization.

00:07:07.642 --> 00:07:09.600
So you're going to end
up with some things that

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.720
are shared and are going to
be big line items on your bill

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.110
that you may want to go
get more detail about.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.090
So for Bluecore, what
we really wanted is

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.220
we really wanted to know
what is our cost per team,

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:21.423
per engineering team.

00:07:21.423 --> 00:07:23.340
We want to be able to
have that accountability

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.550
to say, hey, these five
people on data science,

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:27.900
your bill went up last month.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.790
Can you go investigate
if that's expected

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:30.998
or if there's a problem here?

00:07:30.998 --> 00:07:32.970
Or hey, this email
feature that just got

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:35.427
turned on by this team,
maybe it turns out

00:07:35.427 --> 00:07:38.010
that that's more expensive than
we thought it was going to be.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.372
Maybe we need to optimize that.

00:07:40.372 --> 00:07:42.580
We also wanted to know what
our cost is per customer.

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:45.103
So we are software as
a service application.

00:07:45.103 --> 00:07:46.770
It'd be really nice
for us to look, hey,

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:48.330
for some of our
large customers, what

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:51.360
does it cost us to actually
provide the service to them?

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.595
Do we have the
right pricing model?

00:07:53.595 --> 00:07:56.525
And our finance team really
wants to know projections.

00:07:56.525 --> 00:07:57.900
It turns out that
they would like

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.108
to know six months from now,
what is our Google Cloud

00:08:00.108 --> 00:08:00.960
bill going to be?

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:02.670
They're really unhappy
when it's changing

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:04.010
all the time, which it is.

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:05.760
So they want to be
able to have some model

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.260
to look forward and
say, great, what

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:09.630
is it going to cost us in
six months, or the next year?

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:12.270
What will it cost us if we sign
this particularly large deal

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:15.810
with this particular customer
that has some specific needs?

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:18.660
Can we negotiate or
customize their contract?

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.715
We need to know what it's
going to cost us to serve them.

00:08:21.715 --> 00:08:23.840
So these things are somewhat
organization specific.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.270
What Bluecore wants
for the cost per team

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:29.360
or our cost per customer is
not what you are going to need.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.840
And but the point is these
are fairly generic things.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.860
And you can sort of
tweak these things

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:38.610
to suit your organization.

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:41.549
So as an overview, here's
what we've done at Bluecore

00:08:41.549 --> 00:08:42.610
to break down our bill.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:44.923
Our step one is put
everything into BigQuery.

00:08:44.923 --> 00:08:46.590
We already use BigQuery
a heck of a lot,

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:47.790
so that's a convenient place.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:49.415
You could put it into
some other thing,

00:08:49.415 --> 00:08:51.420
but you need to put
the data in one place

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:53.230
so you can go and analyze it.

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:55.890
We then combine Google's
other data sources

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:57.910
to get some additional
levels of detail.

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:00.160
And we'll talk about more
specific details about which

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:02.230
ones in a moment.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:03.780
And then step three
is four things

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:05.155
where we don't
have enough detail

00:09:05.155 --> 00:09:07.050
from those existing
Google data sources,

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:08.850
we actually go and
instrument the code

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:12.170
to add that level of detail.

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:13.970
The end result is
you can make metrics

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:15.830
that matter to your
team, For Bluecore, it's

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:18.350
that cost per customer or that
cost per engineering team.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:20.990
But maybe for you,
it's something else.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:24.200
You can do that by customizing
the existing data that

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:27.100
is available on Google Cloud.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:28.960
So as a quick example--

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:30.790
sorry for this graph
isn't super visible.

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:33.290
But here's a quick example of
what this actually looks like.

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:35.680
This is our cost by type
over the last 20 days.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:37.480
This is effectively
our engineering team.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.400
So you can see this top
line at the very top.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:41.507
This is the email cost.

00:09:41.507 --> 00:09:43.840
And you can see that in
February 26, it was fairly high.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:45.923
And then it actually
decreased because we deployed

00:09:45.923 --> 00:09:47.590
some sort of optimization.

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:49.173
And then the next
line here is events.

00:09:49.173 --> 00:09:51.048
So you can see for
Bluecore, those are really

00:09:51.048 --> 00:09:52.230
the two big cost drivers.

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:56.050
It's ingesting data onto our
platform, which is events,

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:57.717
and then sending
emails, which is email.

00:09:57.717 --> 00:09:59.217
There's a bunch of
these other teams

00:09:59.217 --> 00:10:00.808
and other line
items at the bottom.

00:10:00.808 --> 00:10:02.350
From a cost optimization
perspective,

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:04.100
we can kind of ignore
them because they're

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:06.370
a small part of our bill.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:09.100
Here's a table that was created
by somebody in our finance team

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:09.918
using this data.

00:10:09.918 --> 00:10:11.960
Again, I can't actually
show you any of the data.

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:14.990
So this is just the column
headers off of this table.

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:17.252
This is showing the 25
lowest margin customers,

00:10:17.252 --> 00:10:18.460
and I really love this table.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:21.520
It shows the brand name, the
monthly recurring revenue

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:25.410
for last month, the total
email and event cost, and then

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:26.433
a computed margin.

00:10:26.433 --> 00:10:27.850
That is an email
and event margin.

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:31.050
That is how profitable or
unprofitable it is, as well as

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:32.235
the total email [? sense. ?]

00:10:32.235 --> 00:10:33.610
The thing I love
about this table

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:36.152
is that our finance team created
this without the engineering

00:10:36.152 --> 00:10:37.660
team doing anything.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:41.320
All we've done is we've
provide this cost per customer

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:43.330
with this email and event cost.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:45.210
And they combined it
with the existing data

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.710
about how much revenue we're
getting per customer to produce

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:48.520
this table.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.020
And this table has
been super useful

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:52.312
because we go and look at
this and be like, hey, why is

00:10:52.312 --> 00:10:53.660
customer x costing us so much?

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:54.678
That's really weird.

00:10:54.678 --> 00:10:57.220
And then inevitably, when that
happens, we go and look at it.

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:00.040
And it turns out there's some
sort of configuration issue,

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:02.710
or they're using some particular
weird part of the product

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:04.030
in a way that we didn't expect.

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:05.770
And we can go and actually
fix that and actually improve

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:06.437
these problems.

00:11:06.437 --> 00:11:08.020
So this has been
super, super helpful.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:10.123
And again, the part I love
is the engineering team

00:11:10.123 --> 00:11:11.290
had nothing to do with this.

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:13.550
The finance team actually
created this themselves.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.550
So this is just an example
of the kind of things

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:16.925
that you can do
once you actually

00:11:16.925 --> 00:11:21.000
instrument your bill to the
level of detail that you need.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.460
All right, let's talk about
how we can actually do this.

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:25.710
First, let's talk about the
existing cost management

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:27.580
tools that Google provides.

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:29.580
The first thing that you
really should look at--

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.710
and certainly, I look
at-- for any cost problem

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:33.905
is the Google Cloud
billing reports.

00:11:33.905 --> 00:11:36.280
Bluecore has been a Google
Cloud customer for long enough

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:38.630
that this billing report
did not used to exist.

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:39.610
It does now exist.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:40.750
It's actually useful.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:42.760
If you haven't played
with it, you should.

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:45.290
It looks like the screenshot
shown here on the right.

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:48.880
It will show you your cost
per project, per service,

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:52.680
per line item or SKU, sliced
over different time ranges.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:55.000
It lets you slice and
dice this how you want.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.010
It's pretty useful.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:59.410
The part that is
still unfortunate,

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:00.910
though, is that
there are things,

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:02.980
like this big blue
bar here at the top,

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:06.280
that it can't provide you any
more detail than the line item

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:07.090
and project level.

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:10.030
So why is this big
blue bar so high?

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:12.735
We're going to have to go do
some work to figure that out.

00:12:12.735 --> 00:12:14.110
So the first step
to figuring out

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:16.780
why the heck this single
line item is so large

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:19.730
is to get the actual raw data.

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:21.580
This is where the
billing export comes in.

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:24.550
This lets you get your detailed
cost data exported directly

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:25.480
to BigQuery.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:27.130
There's also a JSON export.

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:29.440
I don't find that
to be as useful.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:31.720
If there's one thing that
you take away from this,

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:33.950
you should go turn
this feature on today.

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:35.680
The reason is you
can't go back in time

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:39.350
and get the detailed billing
data for data in the past.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:42.640
So if you are going to want to
do cost analysis at some point

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:44.380
in the next, say,
year and are going

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:47.050
to want to look back at
cost data from today,

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:48.792
you need to go
turn this on today.

00:12:48.792 --> 00:12:50.250
It probably won't
cost you anything

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:53.030
if you're not using
BigQuery very much anyway.

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:55.530
What it gives you is it gives
you this sort of detailed data

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:56.613
that's shown on the right.

00:12:56.613 --> 00:12:59.100
We're going to talk a little
bit about it in a moment,

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:00.782
about the actual
Google Cloud bill.

00:13:00.782 --> 00:13:02.490
And there's some sort
of schema for this.

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:04.380
There's lots of documentation.

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:07.492
OK, so what do you get when you
actually-- if you're actually

00:13:07.492 --> 00:13:09.450
working with this data,
how should you actually

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:09.992
work with it?

00:13:09.992 --> 00:13:13.020
A few tips that are useful
to know-- first thing

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:14.190
is Google bills you.

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:15.900
The Google's
official billing time

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:19.740
is Pacific time days, so
Mountain View standard time.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:22.140
You can convert the billing
data into this time zone

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:26.460
by using this BigQuery
function here, TIMESTAMP_TRUNC.

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:29.190
Take the usage start time, and
convert it into this America

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:30.525
Los Angeles time zone.

00:13:30.525 --> 00:13:33.150
Now you'll get days that are the
same days that Google actually

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:34.680
uses, which is
useful when you want

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:36.573
to compare this to your bill.

00:13:36.573 --> 00:13:37.740
Some of the data is delayed.

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:38.740
It's a small percentage.

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:39.920
It keeps decreasing.

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:41.670
Last I looked before
making this talk,

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:44.370
in February, it was
something like 0.1%.

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:46.860
Usually, that data is
delayed only by a day or two.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:49.570
Sometimes it's a little
bit more, but not much.

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:52.840
The point here is if you
want to use this data,

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:55.890
you're going to have to
process chunks of time,

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.350
or reprocess it, or delay
your processing a little bit

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:01.200
to make sure that you
actually capture all of it.

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:03.078
Tax and corrections
show up much later.

00:14:03.078 --> 00:14:04.620
Tax shows up at the
end of the month.

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:06.450
If you have
corrections or credits,

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:07.770
they will show up way later.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:09.370
This can also screw
up your analysis.

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.245
So you may end up having
to, again, reprocess

00:14:11.245 --> 00:14:14.903
this data multiple times to
sort of get the correct view.

00:14:14.903 --> 00:14:16.320
And the other thing
is this schema

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.070
has human descriptions in it.

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:23.040
It has a sku.description
and a product.description.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:23.970
And these can change.

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:25.887
The most recent change
was Google changed this

00:14:25.887 --> 00:14:29.130
from calling it
standard instance CPU

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:32.470
and standard instance RAM
to predefined instance

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:34.140
CPU and RAM.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:36.780
This is totally fine if you're
doing some ad hoc analysis

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:39.088
and just looking at what
was the cost last week.

00:14:39.088 --> 00:14:41.380
If you're trying to do some
analysis over, say, a year,

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:44.430
you're going to want to use the
ID fields that Google provides.

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:45.960
And now the problem
is that those

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:48.350
aren't human understandable.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:49.110
OK.

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:50.950
What does it actually look like?

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:53.130
So let's go and
recreate this graph

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:54.690
from the Google billing report.

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:57.880
This is showing us the top
SKUs by day this month.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:00.630
These are their top Google
Cloud billing line items

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:03.565
for the current month
broken down each day.

00:15:03.565 --> 00:15:05.940
So that was what the screenshot
was that we showed before

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:07.112
on the left hand side.

00:15:07.112 --> 00:15:09.570
And on the right hand side is
this sort of BigQuery results

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:12.180
that we want, which have
the day, as well as then

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:15.090
the actual line item here,
like the frontend instance,

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:18.370
data store, Cloud data
store read operations.

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:19.930
What does this look like?

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:21.090
Here's the example query.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:22.600
You don't have to understand it.

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:24.300
I am going to walk through
a couple of the parts that

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:25.717
are somewhat
important to give you

00:15:25.717 --> 00:15:28.260
a sense about what the actual
raw Google billing data looks

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:29.650
like.

00:15:29.650 --> 00:15:31.260
The first thing
here is this day.

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:35.850
This is this magic conversion
to Google standard billing days.

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:38.370
So TIMESTAMP_TRUNC, that
you should start time

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:40.412
to this America Los
Angeles time zone.

00:15:40.412 --> 00:15:42.120
Again, we want to
actually go and compare

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:44.288
to our actual Google Cloud bill.

00:15:44.288 --> 00:15:46.080
You're going to want
to convert to that day

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:48.200
if you want to be able do that.

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:51.170
This SKU description, this is
this human readable description

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:53.250
of that line item.

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:55.800
And then we want to sum
our cost in this sort

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:59.190
of magic expression here with
this coalesce unnest thing.

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:02.100
It's BigQuery magic to say,
hey, take the credits that

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:04.380
are attached to that line
item, and just sum them

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:05.350
all up as well.

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:11.150
So when you have credits,
like say the sustained usage

00:16:11.150 --> 00:16:12.678
discount in Compute
Engine, there

00:16:12.678 --> 00:16:14.970
can be multiple credits
attached to a single line item.

00:16:14.970 --> 00:16:16.803
We want to add all of
those credits together

00:16:16.803 --> 00:16:22.040
to get the actual cost after
the credits have been applied.

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:24.478
We want to take the sum of
days since the beginning

00:16:24.478 --> 00:16:25.020
of the month.

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:27.510
So that's this timestamp
trunc to the month

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:28.790
and this partition time magic.

00:16:28.790 --> 00:16:30.100
Again, you don't have
to understand this.

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:32.000
And then we want to
sum all these together.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:34.250
The final result is going
to look something like this.

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:37.280
This is Bluecore's actual
top line items for March 16.

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:39.920
Frontend instances--
that's App Engine CPU.

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:43.280
Google Cloud Data Store read
operations, backend instances--

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:45.020
that's also App Engine CPU.

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:46.820
And then BigQuery
flat rate annual,

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:51.040
we're a big BigQuery user, so
we pay for flat rate pricing.

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:51.540
Great.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:53.880
So now we've just recreated
what the billing report already

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:54.380
gives us.

00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:55.270
So who cares?

00:16:55.270 --> 00:16:56.010
What's the point?

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.680
Well, the point is
we can now actually

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:00.190
go and do additional analysis.

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:01.650
And one of the things
that you might want to do,

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:03.358
if you're a little
bit crazy, is actually

00:17:03.358 --> 00:17:04.996
go reproduce your exact invoice.

00:17:04.996 --> 00:17:07.079
Now this sounds a little
silly, and I admit it is.

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:09.750
It doesn't serve a whole
lot of practical purpose,

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:13.440
but it is very useful to be
able to verify that whatever

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:15.300
conversions or
analysis you're doing

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:18.079
is actually correct by matching
your Google Cloud bill.

00:17:18.079 --> 00:17:19.980
If you want to do
this craziness,

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:22.319
Google provides you some tools
to make it actually easy.

00:17:22.319 --> 00:17:25.530
First is it provides this
invoice.month field, which

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:27.720
gives you a year and month
string, which is exactly

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:30.750
which invoice that particular
record is going to end up

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:32.130
on from Google's bill.

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:33.218
So you can group by that.

00:17:33.218 --> 00:17:35.010
The second thing you're
going to want to do

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:38.490
is you need to round
your cost and credits

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:42.240
to two decimal places at the
line item or the SKU level.

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:45.540
So this is this round
to two decimal points

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:47.820
BigQuery expression there.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:51.990
Finally, the export data will
show tax as one line item

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.318
at the beginning
of the next month,

00:17:54.318 --> 00:17:55.860
whereas if you look
at your PDF bill,

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:58.720
it will actually show you
your tax on separate projects.

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:01.410
So if you're comparing your PDF
bill to your BigQuery results

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:02.460
and really want it
to add up, you're

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:04.285
going to have to do
a little bit of math.

00:18:04.285 --> 00:18:06.660
And if you do this, you actually
get exactly to your bill

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:10.080
down to the exact penny
since December 2018.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:14.080
There's a document in Google's
billing documentation,

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:16.650
which will show you some
exact query examples.

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:18.760
Again, is this
actually that useful?

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:20.140
It's useful for
debugging things.

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:21.850
But for the most part,
if you don't do this,

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:24.058
you're going to get within
the nearest dollar anyway.

00:18:24.058 --> 00:18:26.730
So you don't necessarily have to
do this, unless you're like me,

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.490
and are kind of anal retentive,
and want to really make sure

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:31.630
that you got it right.

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:33.630
OK, so now, what
can we do with this?

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:36.352
We have our raw billing
data export into BigQuery.

00:18:36.352 --> 00:18:38.310
We would now want to
actually go and look at it

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:40.920
and be able to
produce some reports.

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:42.690
I suggest that you
use Data Studio.

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:45.420
Again, it's built into Google
Cloud, available for you

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:46.560
for free.

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:48.510
This is not a talk
about Data Studio.

00:18:48.510 --> 00:18:50.940
I'm not going to tell you how
to use it or show it to you.

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:53.370
There's lots of other talks
and resources about this.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:56.860
Again, the billing documentation
itself provides some examples.

00:18:56.860 --> 00:18:59.670
But the point here is you can
now create custom dashboards

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:00.808
from this billing data.

00:19:00.808 --> 00:19:02.850
The reason this is useful
is that there sometimes

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:07.108
are things in the built-in
Google billing tool

00:19:07.108 --> 00:19:09.150
that you may want to come
back to multiple times.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:12.450
Maybe you want to look at here's
the top cost for our production

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:15.930
project, combined with some
cost for some of our top line

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:17.650
items in a separate chart.

00:19:17.650 --> 00:19:19.530
You can create a
dashboard in Data Studio

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:20.850
just by pointing and clicking.

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:23.130
Save that as a link,
share it with others,

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:25.000
look at it every single day.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:27.000
So again, this is
still the same data

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.070
that's available in that
billing report thing.

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:30.662
But now you can
customize it to look

00:19:30.662 --> 00:19:32.370
at stuff that is
actually relevant to you

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:33.930
and make that easier.

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:36.060
I also just find Data
Studio to be a useful tool

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:37.770
to know about if you
need to do some ad

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:40.260
hoc visualization of BigQuery
stuff or other data sources

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:40.890
anyway.

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:43.390
So it's useful to play with.

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:44.200
OK.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.990
So this is basically the
built-in Google Cloud billing

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:47.672
tools.

00:19:47.672 --> 00:19:49.630
So now let's talk about
what other data sources

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:52.370
we might want to use to
get more detail on this.

00:19:52.370 --> 00:19:53.890
So as a reminder,
the point here is,

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:55.682
we have these big line
items that are left.

00:19:55.682 --> 00:19:57.940
So, for example,
this big blue bar--

00:19:57.940 --> 00:19:59.410
what the heck is in this thing?

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:02.510
We'd like to know more detail
about where that's coming from.

00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:06.370
So there are other
data sources about what

00:20:06.370 --> 00:20:08.650
you're using on Google Cloud
that you can go and pull

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:09.620
some of this from.

00:20:09.620 --> 00:20:11.920
Some examples on App Engine,
you have request logs

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:14.530
that tell you every request
that came into your application.

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:16.155
For BigQuery, there
are query logs that

00:20:16.155 --> 00:20:18.580
tell you about every
query that got executed.

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:20.350
For Pub/Sub, there are
Stackdriver metrics

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:22.210
that tell you about
the total bytes going

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:24.210
into and out of your topics.

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:26.080
For Kubernetes
Storage and Compute,

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:27.940
there are usage exports
that will tell you

00:20:27.940 --> 00:20:31.860
various granularities of
usage data about those things.

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:34.600
And so we can go and combine
these existing usage data

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:36.940
things with our bill
to find out what

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:39.680
are in those big line items.

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:41.080
So here's the plan.

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:42.730
We're going to get
the usage per day

00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:44.260
for one of these
large line items

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:46.417
that we want to
understand in more detail.

00:20:46.417 --> 00:20:48.250
We're then going to
combine that information

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:53.190
with our official bill to get
a usage metric per dollar.

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:55.510
And then we're going to
produce an annotated bill that

00:20:55.510 --> 00:20:58.090
has that original
data from Google a lot

00:20:58.090 --> 00:21:00.160
but broken out into
these separate categories

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:02.710
with this additional annotation.

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:04.410
So the exact way
you do this is going

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:06.603
to depend a little
bit on the resource

00:21:06.603 --> 00:21:07.770
that you want to break down.

00:21:07.770 --> 00:21:10.145
I'm going to talk through what
we did for App Engine CPU,

00:21:10.145 --> 00:21:13.140
just because that is for Google,
this is the largest line item.

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:14.040
Or for us, Siri.

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:16.915
For Bluecore, the largest
line item that we have.

00:21:16.915 --> 00:21:18.540
The largest line item
we have currently

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:20.610
is App Engine frontend
instances, which

00:21:20.610 --> 00:21:22.230
is CPU time on App Engine.

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:24.990
We started five or six years ago
as an App Engine application.

00:21:24.990 --> 00:21:28.080
It's still our biggest
line item today.

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:29.050
So how do we do this?

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:31.550
App Engine has these requests
logs, and they look like this.

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:33.717
You do not need to understand
what is on this slide,

00:21:33.717 --> 00:21:36.830
but the point is there's a whole
lot of data in this existing

00:21:36.830 --> 00:21:37.865
request log.

00:21:37.865 --> 00:21:39.240
And when we were
looking at this,

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:41.073
we noticed, hey, there's
this line item here

00:21:41.073 --> 00:21:43.170
that says mega cycles 29.

00:21:43.170 --> 00:21:44.833
What the heck is that?

00:21:44.833 --> 00:21:46.500
If you look it up in
this documentation,

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:49.750
it says something like this is
a suggestion of how much CPU

00:21:49.750 --> 00:21:51.030
is used by this request.

00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:54.700
But it's actually
not very accurate.

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:56.830
User be warned.

00:21:56.830 --> 00:21:58.420
So we wanted to
know, OK, well, this

00:21:58.420 --> 00:22:00.190
says it may or may
not be accurate.

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:02.210
What can we do with it?

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:04.000
So we went to Data
Studio and produced

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:07.280
a visualization of our total
App Engine cost at the top.

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:10.330
So this is the data from the
Google Cloud billing export

00:22:10.330 --> 00:22:13.807
per day for this one line item.

00:22:13.807 --> 00:22:15.640
And then we went and
summed this mega cycles

00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:17.230
value out of the
App Engine request

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:19.780
logs over that same time period.

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:23.310
And so what do you see when
you see these two graphs?

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:25.530
To me, that's pretty damn close.

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:27.030
If you look at these
two blobs here,

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:29.560
you'll notice that they're not
actually completely identical.

00:22:29.560 --> 00:22:31.050
There's a little bit of
difference between these two

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:32.080
things.

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:33.510
But that's pretty close.

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:34.080
So fine.

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:35.330
Maybe there's some error here.

00:22:35.330 --> 00:22:36.720
Maybe we're off by 1%.

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:38.250
Maybe we're off by 5%.

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:39.060
I'm not sure.

00:22:39.060 --> 00:22:40.560
But this seems like
this is probably

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:42.690
a useful data source for us.

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:44.790
And in some
experimentation that we've

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:46.620
done by fixing some
things, we've sort of

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:49.080
verified that it is close.

00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:50.110
So now what do we do?

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:52.350
Well, what we can do
is sum the mega cycles

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:55.460
per day out of this App
Engine log to get a total.

00:22:55.460 --> 00:22:57.960
And then we can compute what
the cost per mega cycle per day

00:22:57.960 --> 00:22:58.460
was.

00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:03.252
So 10,000 mega cycles per
dollar, something like this.

00:23:03.252 --> 00:23:05.460
Now we can actually compute
the cost for each request

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:07.980
because each request has
this mega cycles value on it.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:09.930
And we can aggregate
these URL pads

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:13.080
by team and by customer to
break down the annotation

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:15.162
to get that level of
detail that we want.

00:23:15.162 --> 00:23:16.870
There's going to be
some stuff left over.

00:23:16.870 --> 00:23:18.870
And in our case, we just
account this to unknown

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:21.370
because we're not going to
get 100% of our coverage.

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:22.290
And again, that's OK.

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:23.910
That's one of the
points here, is

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:26.950
we don't need this to be
completely 100% accurate.

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:31.403
We just need it to be like
90% accurate or 95% accurate.

00:23:31.403 --> 00:23:32.570
So what does this look like?

00:23:32.570 --> 00:23:33.380
Well, we end up--

00:23:33.380 --> 00:23:34.060
oh, sorry.

00:23:34.060 --> 00:23:35.477
The next part, I
should point out,

00:23:35.477 --> 00:23:37.360
is this is actually
a relative cost.

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:40.870
I don't actually know how much
does a mega cycle cost you?

00:23:40.870 --> 00:23:41.530
I don't know.

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:42.730
Google doesn't tell us.

00:23:42.730 --> 00:23:44.690
It doesn't really make sense.

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:47.350
But as a relative
usage, a fraction

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:50.570
of our usage of App
Engine resources,

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:51.502
it's a useful measure.

00:23:51.502 --> 00:23:53.710
And there's lots of other
resources that sort of look

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:55.550
like this as well,
where you can say, well,

00:23:55.550 --> 00:23:57.640
this is a percentage of
our cost for this day.

00:23:57.640 --> 00:24:00.280
And you can break
it out that way.

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:03.090
So what this looks like is this
proves the table that has, say,

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:03.880
each day.

00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:06.530
In this case, this
is March 18th.

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:09.510
A URL path or a URL
prefix, and then a cost

00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:11.760
that it cost us for that
particular URL prefix.

00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:17.870
So when we've gone and done
this for Bluecore's bill,

00:24:17.870 --> 00:24:21.950
our App Engine CPU annotation
gets us about 38% of our bill.

00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:23.960
We get that sort of
team customer breakdown

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:25.167
for 38% from that.

00:24:25.167 --> 00:24:27.500
We've also had to go and do
this annotation for BigQuery

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:28.000
queries.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:30.220
Like I said, that was our
number four line item.

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:31.970
So we do the sort of
same thing by looking

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:33.560
at the BigQuery query log.

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:35.600
And we've also done this
for Data Store storage

00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:39.260
by looking at this Data Store
storage export data or usage

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:41.602
data that we then export
out of Data Store.

00:24:41.602 --> 00:24:43.310
When we do it for
these three line items,

00:24:43.310 --> 00:24:44.727
we now have 49%
of our bill that's

00:24:44.727 --> 00:24:47.000
left where we don't have
this team and customer

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:48.067
level of detail.

00:24:48.067 --> 00:24:49.400
So this isn't quite good enough.

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:53.180
We got 51%, but we still need to
know what's in some of this 49%

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:55.760
before we know that we have
an accurate enough estimate

00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:58.290
of our costs per
team or per customer.

00:24:58.290 --> 00:24:59.825
So what can we do now?

00:24:59.825 --> 00:25:01.700
We're going to have to
go instrument our code

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:06.040
to get more detail about what's
in that remaining bucket.

00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:06.860
So here's our plan.

00:25:06.860 --> 00:25:07.900
We're going to go and
instrument our code.

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:10.838
We're going to go and instrument
our application in some way.

00:25:10.838 --> 00:25:12.130
We're going to admit that data.

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:13.690
In our case, we
admit it in logs,

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:16.480
which then end up in BigQuery
through the Stackdriver logging

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:17.372
export.

00:25:17.372 --> 00:25:19.330
And then we're going to
go aggregate this usage

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:22.600
data by day, which will then
look like what we did before

00:25:22.600 --> 00:25:24.065
with Google's own data sources.

00:25:24.065 --> 00:25:25.690
We're going to get
some amount of usage

00:25:25.690 --> 00:25:28.900
for these additional line items
on a daily basis, which we

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:32.310
can then combine with the bill.

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:36.030
So as an example, we did this
for Data Store gets and puts.

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:38.340
Like I said, we're an
App Engine application.

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:40.510
This is another large
line item for us.

00:25:40.510 --> 00:25:43.515
It was the original sort of
storage layer for App Engine.

00:25:43.515 --> 00:25:46.140
And the way you get charged for
it is you get charged for every

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:48.870
get you do and every put you
do against this operation.

00:25:48.870 --> 00:25:50.700
So we want to count
how many we have.

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:52.830
So we have to go and
wrap in our application

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:54.630
all the get and
put calls to count

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:56.470
how many actually happened.

00:25:56.470 --> 00:26:00.357
We then log the counts at
the end of each web request,

00:26:00.357 --> 00:26:02.690
so that we can find out for
that particular request, how

00:26:02.690 --> 00:26:03.890
many did we actually do.

00:26:03.890 --> 00:26:06.740
And then we aggregate these in
BigQuery using its JSON extract

00:26:06.740 --> 00:26:10.245
function which lets you pull
fields out of JSON payloads.

00:26:10.245 --> 00:26:11.870
So the log that we
emit looks something

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:13.520
like this, where
we have a counts

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:15.770
section with the various
counts we care about.

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:19.775
In this case, this is data
store gets and data store puts.

00:26:19.775 --> 00:26:21.650
And then we also attach
this namespace thing.

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:23.210
So this is from
our application, we

00:26:23.210 --> 00:26:25.040
have a concept of
a namespace, which

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:26.940
basically maps to the customer.

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:29.900
And so this is why we actually
do this on a per request basis,

00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:32.480
is so that we can get back down
to that level of granularity

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:34.130
of a single customer.

00:26:34.130 --> 00:26:36.050
And then we can run
BigQuery queries

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:38.760
across our logs to basically
sum these up and aggregate those

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:39.260
together.

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:43.080
So what does this look like?

00:26:43.080 --> 00:26:45.122
Well, now we can get a
table that looks something

00:26:45.122 --> 00:26:47.540
like this, where we produce
for a single day in a URL path

00:26:47.540 --> 00:26:51.620
or prefix, how many
gets and puts we did.

00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:53.540
These are absolute costs.

00:26:53.540 --> 00:26:55.950
Google tells us how much
these actually cost.

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:58.200
So now these just translate
directly into dollars.

00:26:58.200 --> 00:26:59.900
So this is actually a little
bit easier than the case

00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:02.540
where you only have a relative
cost metric, where you have

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:03.832
sort of a fraction of usage.

00:27:03.832 --> 00:27:06.290
This one, we can just basically
do the math and say, great,

00:27:06.290 --> 00:27:08.600
this URL prefix cost
this much because

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:12.680
of the price from Google.

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:14.810
So here's what our
actual detailed cost

00:27:14.810 --> 00:27:15.560
schema looks like.

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:18.410
This is the sort of final output
of all of these series of jobs

00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:19.550
that we've done.

00:27:19.550 --> 00:27:21.540
This may or may not
be the ideal schema.

00:27:21.540 --> 00:27:22.432
It's worked for us.

00:27:22.432 --> 00:27:24.390
Hopefully, you can use
it for some inspiration.

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:26.432
If you come up with a
better one, please tell me.

00:27:26.432 --> 00:27:27.770
I'd love to know.

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:29.480
So what does this look like?

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:32.690
The first most important
field is this usageDayPacific.

00:27:32.690 --> 00:27:35.270
This matches Google's
billing days.

00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:37.790
So this is this Mountain
View standard time,

00:27:37.790 --> 00:27:40.320
day from our bill.

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:43.560
The next fields we pull out are
the project, service, and SKU

00:27:43.560 --> 00:27:44.610
or line item.

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:46.770
These are the dimensions
from the billing exports.

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:49.680
These are fields that we
group by from Google's

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:51.750
original billing data.

00:27:51.750 --> 00:27:54.718
We then pull out the cost,
usage amount, and credits.

00:27:54.718 --> 00:27:56.760
These are the metrics from
Google's billing data.

00:27:56.760 --> 00:28:00.150
So we sum these out of
Google's existing bill.

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:01.800
The cost is the actual cost.

00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:04.060
Usage amount is some
per service metric.

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:06.090
So for Data Store, it'll
be read operations.

00:28:06.090 --> 00:28:09.720
For Google Cloud Storage,
it'll be gigabytes seconds

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:10.710
or something like this.

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:13.332
And then credits are
the credit value.

00:28:13.332 --> 00:28:14.790
And the key part
that we've done is

00:28:14.790 --> 00:28:17.440
we have two additional custom
dimensions that we've added.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:18.820
So we, again, had
this namespace,

00:28:18.820 --> 00:28:20.450
which for us is
effectively a customer.

00:28:20.450 --> 00:28:22.200
And this cost category,
which is basically

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:24.090
a software component,
which we can then

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:27.000
map back to a particular team.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:31.110
And the constraint
that we try to continue

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:36.300
to make sure we meet here
is that if we sum this table

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:38.040
on some of these
dimensions, that it

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:39.400
adds up back to Google's bill.

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:41.550
So we're adding these
custom dimensions

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:45.412
as additional
detail on the bill.

00:28:45.412 --> 00:28:46.870
So how do we actually
produce this?

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:48.820
So we get some
original billing data.

00:28:48.820 --> 00:28:51.250
So as an example, we
have a particular day,

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:53.660
March 18th, a particular--

00:28:53.660 --> 00:28:56.110
it'll actually be a project
in a SKU, but for simplicity,

00:28:56.110 --> 00:28:57.490
I'm just showing the SKU here--

00:28:57.490 --> 00:28:58.305
datastore gets.

00:28:58.305 --> 00:28:59.930
And we have some sort
of cost for this,

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:02.470
so it cost us
$2,000 on that day.

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:04.390
We then have instrumented
our application

00:29:04.390 --> 00:29:07.690
to produce this usage data
of showing for that day

00:29:07.690 --> 00:29:14.563
some URL path did 2,400 gets,
some URL path did 29,000 gets.

00:29:14.563 --> 00:29:16.480
And again, we can translate
that back to cost.

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:17.897
So we can combine
these two things

00:29:17.897 --> 00:29:20.870
to produce this annotated bill,
which now has three line items.

00:29:20.870 --> 00:29:24.550
So it has one datastore gets
for, let's say, Team A. We have

00:29:24.550 --> 00:29:26.890
some mapping from
these URLs paths

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:31.570
back to these cost
categories or teams

00:29:31.570 --> 00:29:32.950
and how much that actually cost.

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:35.620
We have datastore
gets for Team B.

00:29:35.620 --> 00:29:38.680
And then again, there's
going to be some left over.

00:29:38.680 --> 00:29:41.380
We don't instrument every
single application that we run.

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:43.210
We just try to
instrument the big users,

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:44.460
and so there's some left over.

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:46.440
In this case, say $363.

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:47.480
And again, that's OK.

00:29:47.480 --> 00:29:48.850
We don't need to be perfect.

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:50.740
We just need to get
most of it in order

00:29:50.740 --> 00:29:55.170
to be able to make the right
optimization decisions.

00:29:55.170 --> 00:29:57.620
So now that we've
done this, we managed

00:29:57.620 --> 00:30:00.230
to cover 51% of our
bill just by using

00:30:00.230 --> 00:30:02.880
Google's built-in other
usage data sources.

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:05.720
So we were able to get 51%
of our bill down to this team

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:07.990
and customer level
without necessarily going

00:30:07.990 --> 00:30:09.430
to instrument our application.

00:30:09.430 --> 00:30:10.940
And at Bluecore, we've
then gone and instrumented

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:12.000
a couple of data sources.

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:14.730
So datastore reads and
writes is one example.

00:30:14.730 --> 00:30:17.030
There's a couple of others
that we've done as well.

00:30:17.030 --> 00:30:20.780
That gets us another 24% of our
total bill down to this cost

00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:22.472
category and customer level.

00:30:22.472 --> 00:30:23.930
There's going to
be some left over.

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:26.180
In our case, it's
currently about 25%.

00:30:26.180 --> 00:30:28.610
And in our case, this
percentage continues to grow.

00:30:28.610 --> 00:30:31.380
This remaining bucket
continues to grow over time.

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:34.400
And our approach has been
once it gets too large,

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:36.450
we go and look at it
and figure out what

00:30:36.450 --> 00:30:37.700
do we have to instrument next.

00:30:37.700 --> 00:30:39.765
What usage data
can we import next?

00:30:39.765 --> 00:30:41.390
And the point, again,
here is you don't

00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:42.557
have to be perfect about it.

00:30:42.557 --> 00:30:44.120
We just need to
get it right enough

00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:46.580
and get enough of
the data correct.

00:30:46.580 --> 00:30:51.410
And for us, this is
sort of accurate enough.

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:51.910
Great.

00:30:51.910 --> 00:30:54.243
So now let's talk a little
bit about some of the lessons

00:30:54.243 --> 00:30:57.770
that we've learned through
going and doing this.

00:30:57.770 --> 00:31:00.370
The first one is you really
want to break cost down to teams

00:31:00.370 --> 00:31:03.220
because that is what actually
makes metrics actionable,

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:05.290
is when you can get
the responsibility down

00:31:05.290 --> 00:31:08.200
to a small enough
pool of people.

00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:09.190
What is small enough?

00:31:09.190 --> 00:31:11.290
It's kind of going to
depend on your organization.

00:31:11.290 --> 00:31:14.030
But in Bluecore's case, when it
was 40 engineers and we said,

00:31:14.030 --> 00:31:17.247
hey, datastore costs went up
last week, that was too large.

00:31:17.247 --> 00:31:19.330
Everyone just sort of
looked around and been like,

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:19.872
I don't know.

00:31:19.872 --> 00:31:21.010
It wasn't my problem.

00:31:21.010 --> 00:31:22.690
You need to get it down to a
small enough group of people

00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:25.310
so that when you say that to
someone, they're like, oh,

00:31:25.310 --> 00:31:26.930
actually we did
something last week.

00:31:26.930 --> 00:31:28.570
Maybe we should look at this.

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:34.330
As an example, here's a cost
for one line item over 24 days.

00:31:34.330 --> 00:31:35.770
This starts at zero.

00:31:35.770 --> 00:31:37.870
It goes up to a
question mark number.

00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:40.780
And when I look at this,
I say, maybe something

00:31:40.780 --> 00:31:42.308
happened there at the end.

00:31:42.308 --> 00:31:42.850
I don't know.

00:31:42.850 --> 00:31:44.480
There's a lot of variation.

00:31:44.480 --> 00:31:46.732
So this is what our
bill looked like.

00:31:46.732 --> 00:31:47.440
I can't remember.

00:31:47.440 --> 00:31:49.660
I think this was in January.

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:50.938
Here is that same line item.

00:31:50.938 --> 00:31:52.480
If we look at just
the responsibility

00:31:52.480 --> 00:31:54.920
for one team for
one cost category,

00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:56.420
again, this starts at zero.

00:31:56.420 --> 00:31:57.940
This is the same 24 days.

00:31:57.940 --> 00:32:01.660
And you can now tell, holy crap,
this went up by a factor of 2

00:32:01.660 --> 00:32:03.940
for this particular team.

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:05.962
Maybe we should go
and look at this.

00:32:05.962 --> 00:32:07.420
Sure enough, we
went to look at it.

00:32:07.420 --> 00:32:10.890
Here's that same team's
SKU over 46 days.

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:12.140
We went and fixed the problem.

00:32:12.140 --> 00:32:14.025
The cost went back down
to normal after this.

00:32:14.025 --> 00:32:15.400
So this is just
an example of you

00:32:15.400 --> 00:32:18.280
need to get the metrics to
the level of granularity that

00:32:18.280 --> 00:32:19.930
work for your organization.

00:32:19.930 --> 00:32:22.310
So that you can go and
look at something and say,

00:32:22.310 --> 00:32:24.190
hey, there's definitely
something wrong here.

00:32:24.190 --> 00:32:26.620
It's definitely in
this particular part

00:32:26.620 --> 00:32:28.330
of our product or our software.

00:32:28.330 --> 00:32:30.300
Let's go and fix the problem.

00:32:30.300 --> 00:32:32.300
This actually leads to
another interesting part,

00:32:32.300 --> 00:32:34.250
is in the cloud
when you are using

00:32:34.250 --> 00:32:36.890
a lot of these automatic
scaling features,

00:32:36.890 --> 00:32:39.242
performance bugs actually
become cost bugs.

00:32:39.242 --> 00:32:40.700
And I think this
is actually great.

00:32:40.700 --> 00:32:42.363
This is actually an improvement.

00:32:42.363 --> 00:32:44.780
In the old world when you had
a performance problem, where

00:32:44.780 --> 00:32:46.363
your load went up,
there was some sort

00:32:46.363 --> 00:32:48.770
of error retrying or customer
load when that happened,

00:32:48.770 --> 00:32:50.242
your service just went down.

00:32:50.242 --> 00:32:51.950
So you knew about the
problem right away.

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:54.170
But it also meant that your
application was broken,

00:32:54.170 --> 00:32:56.275
and your service was broken.

00:32:56.275 --> 00:32:58.400
If you're using a lot of
these auto scaling things,

00:32:58.400 --> 00:33:00.300
your service is just
going to continue to work.

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:01.940
It just might cost
you a heck of a lot.

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:04.190
So you do need to keep an
eye on these sorts of things

00:33:04.190 --> 00:33:06.920
to be able to find these
problems before they cost you

00:33:06.920 --> 00:33:08.157
a lot of money.

00:33:08.157 --> 00:33:10.490
There are built-in Google
features for this, by the way.

00:33:10.490 --> 00:33:11.865
There are billing
alerts that you

00:33:11.865 --> 00:33:14.090
can set on various
levels of granularity.

00:33:14.090 --> 00:33:17.650
There are all sorts of
other things you can do.

00:33:17.650 --> 00:33:20.460
Finally, my big point
is, again, make metrics

00:33:20.460 --> 00:33:21.610
that matter for your team.

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:23.520
What has worked for
Bluecore may or may not

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:25.050
work for you and your team.

00:33:25.050 --> 00:33:26.190
But the data is there.

00:33:26.190 --> 00:33:27.160
You can go and do it.

00:33:27.160 --> 00:33:29.760
You can break it down to the
level of granularity you want.

00:33:29.760 --> 00:33:32.550
It is possible to go and
annotate this stuff to figure

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:33.987
out where your usage is going.

00:33:33.987 --> 00:33:35.820
You just have to go do
a little bit of work,

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:38.018
and it's not actually that hard.

00:33:38.018 --> 00:33:39.560
To learn more today,
there's a couple

00:33:39.560 --> 00:33:41.270
of other sessions still left.

00:33:41.270 --> 00:33:45.010
10:20, there's Automated
Cost Optimizations on GCP.

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:48.520
1:15, there's Aligning Cloud
Costs with Service Delivery.

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:50.270
You can go to the cost
management showcase

00:33:50.270 --> 00:33:51.687
and talk to some
of the Google PMs

00:33:51.687 --> 00:33:53.580
who are working on
these sorts of things.

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:55.105
And if you have
questions about it,

00:33:55.105 --> 00:33:56.480
there's the Ask
the Expert booth.

00:33:56.480 --> 00:33:58.522
And definitely go and look
at the cost management

00:33:58.522 --> 00:34:01.538
documentation on Google
Cloud on cloud.google.com.

00:34:01.538 --> 00:34:03.830
It has details about all this
stuff that I talked about

00:34:03.830 --> 00:34:06.200
today, like how to set up and
use the billing export, what

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:07.450
does the scheme actually mean.

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:11.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

